# Target C-II — the complete small-leaf rectangle has common order and inverse-`D` phase width

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only structural strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844`  
**Dependencies:** corrected dual-tree small-leaf rectangle; canonical train displacement cones; canonical global strict-rhombus angular-defect budget  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The corrected rectangle theorem supplies complete train families

\[
L_*\times C_*,
\qquad
|L_*|>0.003D-o(D),
\qquad
|C_*|>0.35D-o(D),
\]

where `L_*` lies in the smallest leaf phase of one dominant exact `pi/3` orbit and
`C_*` lies in the center phase, every leaf train is outer-to-outer, each family is
internally noncrossing, and every cross-pair meets in one strict rhombus.

This note adds two geometric facts.

1. The complete rectangle has one common crossing order along every selected train,
   despite holes and despite the dual-tree origin of the leaf family.
2. After deleting at most half of each shore, every remaining crossing angle is
   uniformly

\[
\boxed{
{\pi\over3}<\theta\le {\pi\over3}+{25320\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\]

The retained subrectangle still has shores

\[
\boxed{|L_0|>0.0015D-o(D),\qquad |C_0|>0.175D-o(D).}
\]

Thus the live `1.843577...<=t<=1.844` band contains an actual common-order
near-triangular train grid, not merely a bipartite crossing graph.

---

## 1. Uniform orientation of every leaf-center crossing

Let the limiting center phase be `phi` and the limiting smallest-leaf phase be
`phi+pi/3` projectively.  Freeze shrinking disjoint windows around those two atoms.
For all sufficiently large members of the sequence, choose real representatives

\[
\alpha_c\quad(c\in C_*),
\qquad
\beta_\ell\quad(\ell\in L_*)
\]

so that

\[
{\pi\over3}<\beta_\ell-\alpha_c<{2\pi\over3}
\tag{PC.1}
\]

for every crossing pair.  The lower inequality is the strict unit-rhombus
triangle-free angle condition; the upper inequality follows from the shrinking phase
windows around an exact `pi/3` orbit.

Write

\[
\delta_{c\ell}:=eta_\ell-\alpha_c-{\pi\over3}>0.
\tag{PC.2}
\]

Because the two families have fixed distinct phase labels, every cross-pair uses the
same orientation.  No physical-grain or disk-boundary hypothesis is needed for this
statement: it is a direct property of the two frozen exact-orbit windows and their
actual crossing rhombi.

---

## 2. Common projection and common crossing order

Canonical train geometry orients a train of unit-edge direction `u` so that every
centerline displacement lies in the strict cone

\[
(\arg u+\pi/3,\arg u+2\pi/3).
\]

Orient the center family canonically and the leaf family in the reverse canonical
orientation, exactly as in canonical Section 76.  Put

\[
\alpha_+=\max_{c\in C_*}\alpha_c,
\qquad
\beta_-=\min_{\ell\in L_*}\beta_\ell,
\qquad
\psi={\alpha_++\beta_-\over2}.
\]

By (PC.1), all selected centerline elementary displacements have positive projection
onto the unit vector `e` of argument `psi`.  Thus every selected train is strictly
`X=e\cdot p` monotone.

At every center-leaf crossing, the determinant sign is the same.  In `(X,Y)`
coordinates, with `Y=(Je)\cdot p`, every train is a graph and every crossing has the
same signed graph crossing: one family passes from below to above the other.

The signed monotone-grid order lemma proved in canonical Section 76 therefore applies
verbatim.  We obtain:

\[
\boxed{
\text{all center trains meet the selected leaf trains in one common order,}
}
\tag{PC.3}
\]

and symmetrically

\[
\boxed{
\text{all leaf trains meet the selected center trains in one common order.}
}
\tag{PC.4}
\]

This conclusion is independent of the dual-tree backbone used to extract `L_*`.
The backbone supplies the complete rectangle; the phase geometry upgrades it to a
true ordered pseudoline grid.

---

## 3. The whole rectangle spends only `O(D)` angular defect

The global strict-rhombus angular-defect theorem gives

\[
\sum_R\delta_R<C_{phase}D,
\qquad
C_{phase}={\pi^2\sqrt3(2+\sqrt3)\over12}.
\tag{PC.5}
\]

Every pair in `C_* x L_*` is a distinct strict rhombus and contributes exactly
`delta_{c ell}`.  Therefore

\[
S:=\sum_{c\in C_*}\sum_{\ell\in L_*}\delta_{c\ell}
<C_{phase}D.
\tag{PC.6}
\]

Let

\[
a=|L_*|,
\qquad b=|C_*|,
\qquad\lambda={S\over ab}.
\]

Using the safe rectangle sizes,

\[
ab>(0.003)(0.35)D^2-o(D^2),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
\lambda<{C_{phase}\over0.00105D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{PC.7}

The complete-product orientation gives more than an average statement.  Set

\[
\alpha^+=\max_c\alpha_c,
\qquad
\beta^-=\min_\ell\beta_\ell,
\]

and write

\[
r_c=\alpha^+-\alpha_c\ge0,
\qquad
q_\ell=\beta_\ell-\beta^-\ge0,
\]

\[
g=\beta^- -\alpha^+-{\pi\over3}>0.
\]

Then exactly

\[
\delta_{c\ell}=g+r_c+q_\ell.
\tag{PC.8}
\]

Consequently

\[
g\le\lambda,
\qquad
{1\over b}\sum_cr_c\le\lambda,
\qquad
{1\over a}\sum_\ell q_\ell\le\lambda.
\tag{PC.9}

---

## 4. Half-shore trimming gives uniform inverse-`D` phase width

By Markov, retain subfamilies

\[
C_0\subset C_*,
\qquad L_0\subset L_*
\]

with

\[
|C_0|\ge b/2,
\qquad |L_0|\ge a/2
\tag{PC.10}
\]

such that

\[
r_c\le2\lambda\quad(c\in C_0),
\qquad
q_\ell\le2\lambda\quad(\ell\in L_0).
\tag{PC.11}
\]

Equation (PC.8) now gives, for every retained crossing,

\[
0<\delta_{c\ell}\le5\lambda
<{5C_{phase}\over0.00105D}+o(D^{-1}).
\tag{PC.12}
\]

The companion exact rational certificate uses

\[
\pi<{355\over113},
\qquad
\sqrt3<{17321\over10000}
\]

and verifies

\[
{5C_{phase}\over0.00105}<25320.
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
0<\delta_{c\ell}<{25320\over D}+o(D^{-1})
\qquad(c\in C_0,\ell\in L_0).
}
\tag{PC.13}
\]

Also

\[
\boxed{
|L_0|>0.0015D-o(D),
\qquad
|C_0|>0.175D-o(D).
}
\tag{PC.14}
\]

The same inequalities show each retained family has internal phase spread
`O(1/D)`, because its `r_c` or `q_ell` values lie in an interval of length at most
`2lambda`.

---

## 5. Why this changes the live structural object

The previous rectangle theorem gave a complete crossing relation but, by itself, did
not provide one common crossing order or an inverse-diameter angular chart.  The
current object is stronger:

```text
>0.0015 D outer-to-outer smallest-leaf trains
x
>0.175 D center trains
complete mutual strict-rhombus crossing
+ one common crossing order on both shores
+ every grid angle = pi/3 + O(1/D)
+ Section-157 two-sided actual-diameter bracing on every leaf cut.
```

This is close to a finite oblique-lattice rectangle, but it is **not** an exact affine
lattice.  The selected train directions may vary by `O(1/D)`, and the rest of the
source and the interfaces remain present.  Therefore the restricted
`C-OBLIQUE-LATTICE-COMPETITOR` theorem cannot be invoked directly.

The next useful target is an interface-stable chart theorem: convert the common-order
`O(1/D)` phase spread into an `O(1)` additive-coordinate error across this rectangle,
then compare neighboring Section-157 cut resultants or construct a local
source-preserving shear that keeps the surrounding source feasible.

---

## 6. Scope audit

- The dual-tree correction remains binding.  No statement that all same-phase O--O
  trains globally form one linear stack is used.
- Common order is proved only for the extracted complete rectangle, using the signed
  monotone-grid argument.
- The global `O(D)` defect budget is spent only on actual rectangle rhombi, so no
  injectivity or face-identification assumption is hidden.
- The `25320/D` constant is deliberately coarse; its only purpose is a certified
  inverse-diameter scale.
- No exact triangle, diameter contradiction, or same-cardinality strict competitor is
  claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only common-order/inverse-`D` refinement of the corrected
small-leaf rectangle. C-II and Level I remain open.
